        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-102236",
        ":question/problem": "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point on the curve $y={x}^{2}-3x+10$ whose tangent has a slope of $-1.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x)={x}^{2}-3x+10.$ The slope of the tangent at the point $x$ is equal to ${f}^{′}(x).$ Computing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-3x+10) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(10) \\\\  & =2x-3\\cdot 1+0 \\\\  & =2x-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now set ${f}^{′}(x)=-1$ and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-3 & =-1 \\\\ 2x & =-1+3 \\\\ 2x & =2 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent is equal to $-1$ at the point where $x=1.$"
      },
