        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-343735",
        ":question/problem": "Find the exact value of the $x$-coordinate of the point on the curve $y=2{x}^{2}+4x-3$ whose tangent has a slope of $1.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x)=2{x}^{2}+4x-3.$ The slope of the tangent at the point $x$ is equal to ${f}^{′}(x).$ Computing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{2}+4x-3) \\\\  & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =2(2x)+4(1) \\\\  & =4x+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now set ${f}^{′}(x)=1$ and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+4 & =1 \\\\ 4x & =-3 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent is equal to $1$ at the point where $x=-\\frac{3}{4}.$"
      },
