        ":question/problem": "Find the **positive** $x$-coordinate of the point on the curve $y={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+14x-6$ whose tangent has a slope of $22.$\n\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-1}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x)={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+14x-6.$ The slope of the tangent at the point $x$ is equal to ${f}^{′}(x).$ Computing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+14x-6) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+2x+14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now set ${f}^{′}(x)=22$ and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+2x+14 & =22 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+2x-8 & =0 \\\\ (3x-4)(x+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,\\,\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the positive $x$-coordinate of the points whose tangent has a slope of $6,$ is\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$"
      },
